\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The kept form is a Schur complement}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let the edge weights be strictly positive and the graph
connected, and let #{K} be a nonempty set of vertices. If
#{K} omits at least one vertex, the block of #{L_w} off #{K}
is positive definite. The \vocabk{kept form}{fdip-00DF} is then
#{u^T S u}, where
##{S=A-BC^{-1}B^T}
is formed from the blocks of #{L_w} with the vertices of #{K}
written first, by the same formula as the
\vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D}. The minimizer is the
unique extension whose Laplacian vanishes off #{K}. For
distinct vertices #{a} and #{b}, the kept form of the function
that equals #{1} at #{a} and #{0} at #{b} equals the
\vocabk{effective conductance}{fdip-00DF} #{c(a,b)}.}

\proof{
\p{The case in which #{K} is the whole vertex set is the energy
itself. Suppose some vertex lies outside #{K}, and write
##{L_w=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\ B^T&C\end{pmatrix}}
with #{C} the block off #{K}. If #{y^T C y=0}, extend #{y} by
#{0} on #{K}. The energy of the extension is a sum of
strictly positive weights times squared edge differences, so
every edge difference vanishes. The graph is connected, and
the extension vanishes on #{K}, so the extension is #{0} and
#{y=0}. Thus #{C} is positive definite.}

\p{For an extension #{(u,y)}, the energy expands as
#{u^T A u+2u^T B y+y^T C y}. Set #{K_\ast=C^{-1}B^T}. Then
##{y^T C y+2u^T B y
=(y+K_\ast u)^T C(y+K_\ast u)
-u^T BC^{-1}B^T u.}
The completed square is nonnegative and vanishes at
#{y=-K_\ast u}. The minimum value is #{u^T S u}. At that
extension the block equation #{C y+B^T u=0} says that the
Laplacian vanishes off #{K}. Positive definiteness makes the
extension unique. The same completion is written in the proof of
\ref{fdip-000D}.}

\p{Now let #{u} equal #{1} at #{a} and #{0} at #{b}, and let
#{v} be its minimizing extension. Then #{L_w v} vanishes off
#{\{a,b\}}. Each generator of #{L_w} is orthogonal to constant
fields, so the two remaining values sum to #{0}:
#{L_w v=(L_w v)(a)\,(\delta_a-\delta_b)}. The energy is #{v^T L_w v}. It receives a contribution only
from #{a} and #{b}. Since #{v(a)=1} and #{v(b)=0}, the energy
equals #{\alpha=(L_w v)(a)}. The extension #{v} is not
constant, so #{\alpha>0}. Subtract a constant
from #{v/\alpha} so that the result #{\phi} is orthogonal to
constant fields. Then #{L_w\phi=\delta_a-\delta_b}, and
\ref{fdip-005U} gives #{\phi=L_w^+(\delta_a-\delta_b)}. Hence
##{(\delta_a-\delta_b)^T L_w^+(\delta_a-\delta_b)
=\phi(a)-\phi(b)=\frac{1}{\alpha}.}
The reciprocal is the energy of #{v}, which is the kept form
of #{u}.}
}
